            <ArticleTitle>Periprostatic Perineurioma, Description of a Case With Unusual Localization.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>55-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.eucr.2016.04.011</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Perineurioma is a rare entity, it is a benign peripheral nerve sheath tumor entirely composed of perineurial cells. A 62-year-old male patient was admitted to our hospital, suffering from scrotal and pelvic pain combined with a severe and continuous pain in his right thigh. A transrectal ultrasound revealed a periprostatic oval lesion of about 5 cm in maximum diameter. A sovrapubic laparotomy was performed with a complete tumor excision. The morphological and immunohistochemical data were most consistent with the diagnosis of perineurioma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
